Technical Lead → Cloud, DevOps, Docker, Kubernetes & Azure
 Complete Interview + Practical Learning Guide
For an experienced Android / React / Next.js / Node.js Technical Lead
1. Your Target Profile
With around 18 years of IT experience and hands-on work in Android, React, Next.js, Node.js, MySQL, AWS, RDS, Nginx and application releases, the goal is not to become a full-time DevOps engineer. The goal is to become a Technical Lead / Solution Architect who understands the complete path from code to production.
Recommended target:
·         Technical Lead → Senior Technical Lead → Solution / Technical Architect → Head of Engineering
2. The Complete Production Flow
1. Developer writes React / Next.js frontend and Node.js backend code.
2. Git stores the source code and tracks every change.
3. DevOps practices define how code is built, tested, released and operated.
4. Docker packages the application and its runtime into a repeatable container image.
5. GitHub hosts the repository and collaboration workflow.
6. GitHub Actions automatically runs build/test/deployment pipelines.
7. Amazon ECR stores Docker images privately.
8. AWS provides the cloud infrastructure.
9. Nginx or an AWS Application Load Balancer receives web/API traffic and routes it.
10. Node.js runs the backend application.
11. Amazon RDS runs the managed MySQL database.
12. CloudWatch collects logs, metrics and alarms.
13. HTTPS protects traffic using TLS certificates.
14. Automated deployment moves a tested version into production without manually copying files.
3. Git
What is Git?
Git is a version-control system. It records code changes so developers can work safely, create branches, review changes and return to previous versions.
Why do we need it?
Without Git, a team may overwrite each other's code, lose changes, or have no reliable history of who changed what.
Example
You create a feature branch such as feature/login, commit your React/Node changes, push the branch to GitHub, create a pull request, get review, merge it to main, and then CI/CD can deploy it.
Interview points
·         Git is distributed version control.
·         Branching isolates work.
·         Commit records a change.
·         Pull/merge brings changes between branches.
·         Tags are useful for release versions.
·         Do not store passwords, API secrets or private keys in Git.
How you can explain your usage
“I use Git for source control and branch-based development. My team raises pull requests, I review code, and approved changes are merged. The merged branch becomes the input to our CI/CD pipeline.”
4. DevOps
What is DevOps?
DevOps is a way of working that connects development and operations. It focuses on automation, repeatable releases, testing, infrastructure, monitoring and fast feedback.
What problem does it solve?
A developer should not have to manually build files, copy them to a server, restart services and hope nothing was missed. DevOps makes these steps repeatable and automated.
DevOps lifecycle
·         Plan
·         Code
·         Build
·         Test
·         Release
·         Deploy
·         Operate
·         Monitor
·         Improve
Interview answer
“For me, DevOps means creating a reliable path from source code to production using version control, CI/CD, containers or repeatable builds, infrastructure automation, security and monitoring.”
5. Docker
What is Docker?
Docker packages an application together with the dependencies and runtime configuration it needs into a container image. A container is an isolated running instance of that image.
Why do we need it?
It reduces the classic “works on my machine” problem. The same image can be tested and then promoted through environments.
Important concepts
Dockerfile: Instructions used to build an image.
Image: Immutable package used to create containers.
Container: Running instance of an image.
Registry: Place where images are stored, such as Amazon ECR.
Port mapping: Makes a container port reachable from outside the container.
Environment variables: Runtime configuration such as API URLs; secrets should be handled securely.
Node.js example
A Node.js API might listen on port 3000 inside the container. Docker can package the Node runtime, package.json and application code. The same image can run locally, in test and in production.
Interview points
·         Container is not the same thing as a virtual machine; containers share the host OS kernel.
·         Images should be small and reproducible.
·         Use multi-stage builds where appropriate.
·         Do not bake secrets into images.
·         Use health checks and graceful shutdown for production services.
6. CI/CD
CI – Continuous Integration
CI automatically validates code after changes: install dependencies, lint, run tests, build and perform security or quality checks.
CD – Continuous Delivery / Deployment
Continuous Delivery keeps software ready to release. Continuous Deployment automatically releases approved changes to production.
Example pipeline
1.       Developer pushes code
2.       GitHub Actions starts
3.       Install dependencies
4.       Run lint/tests
5.       Build React/Next.js or Node.js
6.       Build Docker image
7.       Scan image
8.       Push image to ECR
9.       Deploy to AWS
10.   Run health check
11.   Notify team
Interview question: Why CI/CD?
It reduces manual errors, gives fast feedback, creates repeatable deployments and makes rollback/release procedures easier to standardize.
7. GitHub
GitHub is a platform for hosting Git repositories and collaborating through branches, pull requests, reviews, issues and automation.
How your team can use it
React, Next.js and Node.js repositories live in GitHub. Developers create branches, submit pull requests, reviewers approve them, and merging to main triggers the deployment pipeline.
8. GitHub Actions
GitHub Actions is GitHub's automation platform. A workflow can run when code is pushed, a pull request is opened, a tag is created, or on a schedule.
Typical workflow
GitHub → workflow runner → checkout → install → test → build → Docker build → authenticate to AWS → push to ECR → deploy → health check.
Interview points
·         Workflow: automation definition.
·         Job: group of steps running on a runner.
·         Step: individual command/action.
·         Secret: protected value used at runtime.
·         Artifact: output retained from a workflow.
9. Amazon ECR
Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) is AWS's managed container image registry.
Why do we need it?
After GitHub Actions builds a Docker image, the image needs a secure place from which AWS compute can pull it. ECR provides that registry.
Example: travel-api:1.0.25 is pushed to a private ECR repository. ECS, EKS or another AWS host can pull that exact image.
10. AWS Fundamentals
AWS is the cloud platform on which you can run compute, networking, databases, storage, security, monitoring and managed application services.
Services you should know
IAM: Users, roles and permissions. Prefer roles and least privilege over long-lived access keys.
VPC: Private network boundary containing subnets, route tables and network controls.
EC2: Virtual servers.
RDS: Managed relational database service, including MySQL.
S3: Object storage for files, backups and static assets.
ALB: Application Load Balancer for HTTP/HTTPS routing.
ECR: Container image registry.
ECS: Managed container orchestration using AWS-native tooling.
EKS: Managed Kubernetes control plane.
CloudWatch: Metrics, logs, alarms and operational visibility.
Route 53: DNS service.
ACM: Managed TLS certificates for supported AWS integrations.
Interview architecture
Client → Route 53/DNS → HTTPS → ALB/Nginx → Node.js service → RDS MySQL. Static files can be served through S3/CDN, and CloudWatch provides logs, metrics and alarms.
11. Linux
Linux is important because many AWS workloads run on Linux. A Technical Lead should be comfortable checking processes, ports, logs, disk, memory, permissions and services.
·         ps / top
·         free -h
·         df -h
·         du -sh
·         ss / netstat
·         systemctl
·         journalctl
·         grep
·         tail -f
·         chmod / chown
·         curl
Interview example: “If the API is down, I first check whether the process/container is running, whether the port is listening, application logs, CPU/memory/disk, network/security-group rules, and dependency health.”
12. Nginx
Nginx is a web server and reverse proxy. It can terminate TLS, serve static React files, route requests to Node.js, add headers and control request sizes/timeouts.
Your practical model
Browser → Nginx → React/Next.js static or server application. API request → Nginx → localhost:3000 Node.js API.
Why reverse proxy?
The Node.js application does not need to be directly exposed to the internet. Nginx can receive public traffic and forward it to the internal application port.
Interview points
·         location blocks control routing.
·         proxy_pass forwards requests.
·         TLS can terminate at Nginx.
·         client_max_body_size controls upload size.
·         Caching, compression, headers and timeouts can be configured.
13. ALB – Application Load Balancer
An AWS Application Load Balancer distributes HTTP/HTTPS traffic across healthy targets such as EC2 instances or container services.
Why do we need ALB?
For high availability, multiple application instances can run behind one public endpoint. The ALB performs health checks and routes traffic to healthy targets.
Typical flow: Internet → Route 53 → ALB → target group → Node.js containers/instances.
14. Node.js in Production
Node.js is your application runtime, not the infrastructure platform. Production concerns include environment configuration, logging, graceful shutdown, health endpoints, process/container restart behavior, connection pooling, timeouts and secure error handling.
Useful health endpoints
GET /health can return a simple application status. A deeper readiness check can verify required dependencies such as the database, but it should be designed carefully so a database outage does not create cascading load.
15. Amazon RDS MySQL
Amazon RDS is a managed relational database service. AWS handles many operational tasks such as provisioning, backups and maintenance options, while you remain responsible for schema, queries, application behavior and appropriate security configuration.
Interview points
·         Use private networking where appropriate.
·         Do not expose the database publicly without a strong reason.
·         Use security groups to restrict database access.
·         Use automated backups and define recovery expectations.
·         Monitor CPU, connections, storage and database performance.
·         Use connection pooling and sensible limits in Node.js.
16. CloudWatch
CloudWatch is used for AWS monitoring, logs, metrics, alarms and operational visibility.
Example
If API error rate or latency increases, CloudWatch alarms can notify the team. Application logs can help identify a failing API, database connection issue or unexpected exception.
Interview answer
“Monitoring is not just checking whether the server is alive. I want application logs, infrastructure metrics, health checks and alerts tied to meaningful service conditions.”
17. HTTPS / TLS
HTTPS is HTTP protected by TLS. It encrypts traffic in transit and provides server identity through certificates.
Example: https://api.example.com → certificate → encrypted connection → ALB/Nginx → Node.js.
Interview point: explain certificate issuance/renewal, TLS termination, HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect and secure cookies/headers where applicable.
18. Automated Deployment
Automated deployment means the release process is encoded as a repeatable pipeline rather than depending on manual WinSCP uploads or manual server commands.
Recommended production flow
12.   Pull request review
13.   Merge to protected branch
14.   CI validation
15.   Build application
16.   Build Docker image
17.   Security/quality checks
18.   Push immutable image tag to ECR
19.   Deploy to staging
20.   Smoke/health tests
21.   Approval if required
22.   Deploy production
23.   Monitor
24.   Rollback if health checks fail
For a mature setup, avoid relying only on a mutable 'latest' image tag. Use versioned tags such as a Git commit SHA or release number.
19. Terraform / Infrastructure as Code
Terraform lets you describe infrastructure as code. Instead of manually creating the same VPC, security groups, RDS settings and compute resources repeatedly, you define them in configuration and manage changes through version control.
Why do you need it?
It improves repeatability, reviewability and disaster recovery. A Technical Architect should understand the concept even if a dedicated platform team owns the Terraform repository.
Interview points
·         Infrastructure becomes reviewable like code.
·         State tracks managed resources.
·         Plan shows intended changes.
·         Apply performs changes.
·         Use modules to standardize repeated infrastructure.
·         Protect secrets and state files appropriately.
20. Kubernetes
Kubernetes is a container orchestration platform. It manages containerized workloads across a cluster and provides scheduling, service discovery, scaling, rolling deployments and self-healing mechanisms.
Why do we need Kubernetes?
If you have many containerized services and need automated scheduling, scaling, rolling updates, service discovery and recovery, Kubernetes can provide a standard control model.
Core concepts
Cluster: The Kubernetes environment.
Node: Worker machine that runs workloads.
Pod: Smallest deployable unit; normally contains one main application container.
Deployment: Describes desired replicas and rollout strategy for stateless applications.
ReplicaSet: Maintains the desired number of pod replicas, normally managed by a Deployment.
Service: Stable network endpoint for reaching pods.
Ingress: HTTP/HTTPS routing into services; actual implementation depends on an ingress controller or gateway.
ConfigMap: Non-secret configuration.
Secret: Sensitive configuration; still requires proper encryption/access controls.
Namespace: Logical isolation/grouping within a cluster.
HPA: Horizontal Pod Autoscaler changes replica count based on metrics.
PersistentVolume: Storage abstraction for workloads needing persistence.
Helm: Package manager/template mechanism commonly used for Kubernetes applications.
Example Node.js Kubernetes deployment
GitHub Actions builds Node.js Docker image → pushes it to ECR → Kubernetes deployment references the image → Kubernetes schedules pods → Service provides stable internal access → Ingress/API gateway exposes HTTP/HTTPS routing → HPA can increase replicas → monitoring collects operational data.
Interview question: Kubernetes vs Docker
Docker packages and runs containers. Kubernetes manages containers at cluster scale. Docker solves packaging/runtime consistency; Kubernetes solves orchestration and operational management across many workloads.
21. Amazon EKS
Amazon EKS is AWS's managed Kubernetes service. It provides a managed Kubernetes control plane while your workloads run on worker capacity such as managed node groups or other supported compute models.
What you should know for interviews
·         EKS is Kubernetes on AWS.
·         Understand cluster, node groups, pods, services and ingress.
·         Understand IAM integration and AWS networking at a high level.
·         Know how ECR images are pulled by workloads.
·         Understand autoscaling and rolling deployment concepts.
·         Know where CloudWatch/other observability tooling fits.
You do not need to start by becoming an EKS platform administrator. First learn how an application is deployed and operated on EKS.
22. Azure
Azure is Microsoft's cloud platform. Your goal after learning AWS is to understand equivalent cloud concepts and enough Azure services to discuss architecture and work with an Azure team.
Azure Virtual Machines: Equivalent concept to EC2.
Azure Virtual Network: Equivalent concept to AWS VPC.
Azure Blob Storage: Equivalent concept to S3.
Azure SQL Database: Managed relational database service.
Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS): Managed Kubernetes.
Azure Container Registry (ACR): Container image registry.
Azure Monitor: Monitoring and telemetry.
Microsoft Entra ID: Identity platform; Azure RBAC controls resource access.
Application Gateway / Load Balancer: Traffic distribution and application routing options.
AWS → Azure interview mapping
EC2 ↔ Azure VM | VPC ↔ VNet | S3 ↔ Blob Storage | ECR ↔ ACR | EKS ↔ AKS | CloudWatch ↔ Azure Monitor. Exact capabilities and architecture differ, so these are conceptual mappings, not identical products.
23. Security – What a Technical Lead Must Know
·         Least privilege: give identities only the permissions they need.
·         Never commit passwords, database credentials or private keys.
·         Use secrets management rather than hardcoding credentials.
·         Separate development, staging and production.
·         Restrict RDS network access.
·         Use HTTPS/TLS for external traffic.
·         Patch operating systems and dependencies.
·         Use dependency and container-image scanning.
·         Protect CI/CD credentials and use short-lived credentials where practical.
·         Log security-relevant events and review access.
24. High Availability, Scaling and Backup
High availability
Keep more than one application instance where appropriate and remove single points of failure.
Vertical scaling
Give one machine more CPU/RAM.
Horizontal scaling
Run more application instances/containers.
Backup vs High Availability
High availability helps keep a service running during failures. Backups help recover data. They are different requirements and both matter.
25. Production Troubleshooting Interview Scenario
Question: “Users say the Node.js API is down. What will you check?”
25.   Check DNS and whether the public endpoint resolves correctly.
26.   Check HTTPS/certificate status.
27.   Check ALB/Nginx availability and routing.
28.   Check target health.
29.   Check whether Node.js container/process is running.
30.   Check application logs.
31.   Check CPU, memory and disk.
32.   Check security groups/network connectivity.
33.   Check RDS connectivity and database connection limits.
34.   Check recent deployment changes.
35.   Rollback to the last known-good release if the deployment is the likely cause.
36.   After recovery, identify the root cause and improve monitoring/automation so the same issue is detected earlier.
26. Common Interview Questions and Simple Answers
What is DevOps?
A combination of practices and automation that connects development and operations to build, test, deploy and operate software reliably.
Why Docker?
To package an application and its dependencies consistently so the same build can move between environments.
Why CI/CD?
To automate validation and deployment, reduce manual errors and make releases repeatable.
Why ECR?
To securely store Docker images so AWS workloads can pull known application versions.
Why RDS?
To use a managed relational database service instead of operating the database server yourself.
Why Nginx?
It can serve web content, reverse-proxy APIs, terminate TLS and control traffic.
Why ALB?
To distribute HTTP/HTTPS traffic across healthy application targets.
Why CloudWatch?
To observe logs, metrics and alarms and detect production problems.
Why Kubernetes?
To orchestrate containerized workloads at cluster scale with scheduling, service discovery, scaling and rolling updates.
Why EKS?
To run Kubernetes on AWS with a managed Kubernetes control plane.
Why Terraform?
To define infrastructure as code so infrastructure changes are repeatable, reviewable and version controlled.
Why Azure if I know AWS?
To understand multi-cloud concepts and communicate with Azure-based teams. Deep Azure specialization can come later.
27. How to Present Your Existing Experience
Use your real experience first. Do not claim that you personally administered a technology if another team handled it.
