55.   Deploy the same container image to Kubernetes.
56.   Move the Kubernetes deployment to EKS.
57.   Learn the equivalent Azure architecture and deploy a small version to Azure/AKS later.
29. Recommended Learning Order
1. Linux + networking fundamentals — Ports, DNS, HTTP/HTTPS, processes, logs, permissions, SSH.
2. AWS fundamentals — IAM, VPC, EC2, security groups, RDS, S3, ALB, Route 53, CloudWatch.
3. Docker — Images, containers, Dockerfile, networking, volumes, Compose.
4. CI/CD — GitHub Actions, environments, secrets, approvals, artifacts, deployment strategies.
5. Production operations — Nginx, TLS, health checks, logs, monitoring, backups, rollback.
6. Terraform — Infrastructure as code, state, modules, plan/apply.
7. Kubernetes — Pods, Deployments, Services, Ingress, ConfigMaps, Secrets, HPA, storage, Helm.
8. EKS — AWS networking/IAM + Kubernetes + ECR integration.
9. Azure — Core services, identity, networking, containers, AKS and monitoring.
10. Architecture — HA, scalability, security, disaster recovery, cost, observability and system design.
30. Final Skill Matrix for Your Target Role
Android / Mobile: Strong — Continue architecture, release and app-system knowledge.
React / Next.js: Strong / growing — Deepen SSR, caching, performance and architecture.
Node.js / API: Strong / growing — Deepen scalability, security, observability and service design.
MySQL / RDS: Working — Learn performance, backups, HA and production operations.
AWS: Working → Intermediate/Advanced — Make this a major focus.
DevOps: Basic/Working → Intermediate — Focus on practical automation.
Docker: Learn deeply — Essential for modern deployment.
CI/CD: Learn deeply — Essential for lead-level delivery ownership.
Terraform: Intermediate — Important architecture/IaC skill.
Kubernetes: Intermediate — Understand operations and architecture; later deepen EKS.
Azure: Foundational → Intermediate — Learn after AWS; map concepts rather than duplicating everything.
System Design: High priority — Connect application + infrastructure + security + scaling.
31. The Key Interview Message
The strongest story for your profile is not “I know every DevOps tool.” It is: “I can take a business requirement, design the application, lead React/Next.js/Node.js/mobile development, understand the database and networking, deploy securely to AWS, automate the release process, monitor production, and make architectural decisions about scaling, reliability and cost.”
That is the bridge from an experienced developer/Technical Lead to a Technical Architect.


